  "answer": "Yes—potentially. Under the Manual’s practice, sales figures are not always essential: other metrics may more accurately demonstrate a trade mark’s capacity to distinguish. Examiners must consider the commercial realities of the claimed goods or services. Low turnover does not, by itself, establish that alternative figures will be sufficient. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1/1; TMM/Part23/4/4/1~2]\n\nUseful alternatives identified in the Manual include:\n• Website and social media analytics, funds managed, raised or distributed, and video or podcast viewership figures. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1/1]\n• Website references showing where the trade mark is used, with Australian visitor figures since first use, preferably broken down by period; relevant app download figures; and third-party social media or SEO reports. [TMM/Part23/3/3/4~2]\n\nFor a business with small turnover, the Manual also recognises that long use may compensate, especially where the trade mark has some inherent adaptation to distinguish. Turnover should be assessed against the relevant market and unit cost; unit-cost and market-share information may help support the evidence. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1~1]\n\nThe qualification is that the strength of the evidence must be balanced against the trade mark’s inherent adaptation to distinguish. I cannot tell from these passages alone whether your proposed figures would be sufficient. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1~2]\n\nThese statements describe Manual practice, not legislation. No Act or Regulations passages were supplied."
